In my opinion, the main problem with revenant is that he's too hard to deal with. Unless he's draining someone, he doesn't stay in place (and revealed) nearly long enough for crew to be able to kill him and with the recent bugfix of him no longer randomly dieing, it's become all the more clear that that's the main problem, you just can't kill him unless he's dumb enough to try to use ability/drain in front of 5 people and even then, you're probably going to be able to get away, unless the guy has laser rifle.

I think Overload Lights is fine, it doesn't need to require revenant to befriend a traitor, he does that plenty enough already:
- Blocked by Insulated Gloves;
- Turn Off/Remove lights;
- Can be mitigated by a simple trip to chemist to tell them to give you bicardine or another burn healing chem;
- Requires at least a corpse or two to actually get;

Increasing cooldown doesn't seem like a bad idea, though. But I'm not sure if Revenant needs to have it's only offensive option removed and be changed into a "coop only" antag. I like the fact that you can just start sowing chaos and harming people.

For .NET 6, the console project template was changed to use top-level statements. The code that is created in Program.cs  is:

```
// See https://aka.ms/new-console-template for more information
Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");
```

In earlier versions of .NET and .NET Core, the code that is created in Program.cs is:

```
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

namespace MyApp // Note: actual namespace depends on the project name.
{
    public class Program
    {
        public static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Hello World!");
        }
    }
}
```

Discussion about whether this was a good change began in https://github.com/dotnet/docs/issues/26313 but there was some confusion about what exactly the various up or down votes applied to. This issue provides comments to vote on that clearly describe the options.
